package ferrum:plugin@1.0.0;

// CAPABILITY. Grant name: "edits".
// Consent: "change the text of your files without being asked".

/// Propose a change to the text of the workspace, unprompted.
///
/// The distinction that decides what needs a grant in this world:
///
///   *Unsolicited* effects need a capability. A plugin that decides on its own
///   to organise imports on save is exercising authority; that is this
///   interface.
///
///   *Solicited* responses do not. A code action's edit is returned as a value
///   from an export the host called, about a request the host constructed, and
///   is applied by the host after the user picked it. A quick-fix plugin needs
///   no `edits` grant at all — see `code-actions.resolve`.
///
/// Getting that line right is what stops the grant set inflating until every
/// plugin holds every capability and consent means nothing.
interface edits {
  use types.{edit-proposal, applied, host-error};

  /// Off-path by construction. The host queues the proposal, returns to the
  /// frame loop immediately, applies it on the UI thread at a frame boundary,
  /// and only then resolves. Nothing here can sit between a keystroke and a
  /// frame because the host is never waiting on it when a keystroke arrives.
  ///
  /// Version-exact: if any buffer moved past its `base` the whole proposal is
  /// rejected with `stale`, never partially applied and never rebased.
  apply: async func(proposal: edit-proposal) -> result<applied, host-error>;
}
